---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import CtaCatchDoms from '../components/CtaCatchDoms.astro';
import listings from '../data/listings.json';

const tlds = Object.values(listings.tlds);

const breadcrumb = [
  { name: 'Expired Domains', href: '/' },
  { name: 'Pending Delete Domains', href: '/pending-delete-domains/' },
];

// Standard EPP status codes. They are NOT a sequence: two of them describe a
// suspension that says nothing about deletion, and one is set by the registrar
// rather than the registry.
const eppStatuses = [
  ['redemptionPeriod', 'Set by the registry after deletion. The former registrant can still restore the name by paying a redemption fee. Nobody else can have it yet.', 'Watch, do not act'],
  ['pendingDelete', 'Redemption has passed without a restore. For a gTLD name in this lifecycle the registry normally releases it five calendar days later.', 'The only stage where a backorder has a known date'],
  ['serverHold', 'Set by the registry. The name is not published in the DNS. It can mean a dispute, an abuse action or an administrative hold, and it does not imply deletion.', 'Says nothing about a drop'],
  ['clientHold', 'Set by the REGISTRAR, not the registry, and it also means the name is not in the DNS. Reasons vary and it does not imply deletion either.', 'Says nothing about a drop'],
];

// Labels used by particular ccTLD registries, not generic EPP values. They are
// listed because you will meet them in a WHOIS answer, not because they are
// standard.
const ccLabels = [
  ['quarantine', 'Used by several country-code registries for their own holding period, in place of the gTLD wording. The length is set by each registry.'],
  ['transition', 'Used by some registries while a name moves between states. Not a standard EPP status, and its meaning depends on the registry.'],
];

const faqs = [
  {
    q: 'What does pending delete mean?',
    a: 'It is the last status a domain holds before a registry releases it. The previous owner can no longer recover it and nobody can buy it from them. For generic extensions the window runs about five days, and at the end the name drops and becomes registrable again.',
  },
  {
    q: 'How long does pending delete last?',
    a: 'For a gTLD name in the standard lifecycle it is normally five calendar days, after a redemption period of normally thirty. Country-code registries set their own rules: the duration, the wording, whether a restore is possible and how the name is finally released all depend on the registry, so there is no single answer that covers them.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Can I buy a domain that is pending delete?',
    a: 'Not from the owner, and not through an auction: that stage has passed. What you can do is place a backorder with a drop-catching service, which attempts to register the name the instant the registry releases it. For a contested name several services compete and the one with the fastest connection wins.',
  },
  {
    q: 'What is the difference with an expired domain auction?',
    a: 'An auction happens earlier, while the registrar still controls the name, and the highest bidder takes it over. Pending delete happens after that, once nobody bought it: there is no open auction, only a race at the moment of release. If the service that wins the race has several customers wanting the same name, it may then run a private auction between them, so you can still end up bidding.',
  },
  {
    q: 'How do I know when a name will drop?',
    a: 'The registry status tells you where it is in the lifecycle, and for gTLDs the drop date is predictable from the status. WHOIS and RDAP publish the status, which is how a tracked inventory is built in the first place. Country-code registries vary, and some publish nothing useful at all.',
  },
];

const schema = [
  {
    '@context': 'https://schema.org',
    '@type': 'FAQPage',
    mainEntity: faqs.map((f) => ({
      '@type': 'Question',
      name: f.q,
      acceptedAnswer: { '@type': 'Answer', text: f.a },
    })),
  },
];
---
<BaseLayout
  title="Pending Delete Domains: What the Status Means and How to Catch One"
  description="Pending delete is the last status before a registry releases a domain. What the window is, how it differs from redemption and from an auction, and how backorders and drop catching actually work."
  breadcrumb={breadcrumb}
  schema={schema}
>
  <section class="hero">
    <div class="kicker">Domain lifecycle</div>
    <h1>Pending delete <em>domains</em></h1>
    <p class="lead">
      The last status a name holds before the registry lets go of it. The former owner can no
      longer recover it, there is no open registrar auction at that stage, and when the window
      closes the name goes to whoever is fastest.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="section prose">
    <h2>The lifecycle, in order</h2>
    <p>
      People use expired, dropping and pending delete as if they were the same thing. They are
      four different stages, and only one of them is a race.
    </p>
    <ol>
      <li>
        <strong>Expired.</strong> The registration lapsed. The registrar usually keeps serving
        a parking page and the former owner can renew at the normal price.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Auction.</strong> Some registrars offer the name at auction during that grace
        period; others return it to the registry with no auction at all. Where there is one,
        the highest bidder takes the name over, and this is where history gets expensive. See
        <a href="/expired-domain-auctions/">expired domain auctions</a>.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Redemption.</strong> For a gTLD name deleted into the Redemption Grace Period,
        normally thirty days, during which only the former registrant can restore it by paying
        a redemption fee.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Pending delete.</strong> Normally five calendar days after redemption, under
        that same gTLD lifecycle. Nobody can register the name until the registry releases it,
        and at the end of the window it does. Not every expired name follows this sequence: it depends on the
        deletion, the registry and the extension.
      </li>
    </ol>
    <p>
      That last step is why the status is worth tracking. It is the only point in the lifecycle
      where the drop date becomes predictable, which is what makes a backorder worth placing.
    </p>

    <h2>What each status actually tells you</h2>
    <p>
      These are not four steps in a row. Two of them describe a suspension and tell you nothing
      about a deletion, and one of those is set by the registrar rather than the registry.
      Reading them as a countdown is the most common mistake.
    </p>
    <div class="table-wrap" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="Standard EPP status codes">
      <table class="listing-table" style="min-width:600px">
        <caption class="sr-only">Standard EPP status codes you will meet, and what each one does and does not tell you</caption>
        <thead><tr><th>EPP status</th><th style="text-align:left">What it means</th><th style="text-align:left">What it tells you</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          {eppStatuses.map(([s, m, a]) => (
            <tr>
              <td class="dom">{s}</td>
              <td style="text-align:left; white-space:normal; font-family:var(--ui)">{m}</td>
              <td style="text-align:left; white-space:normal; font-family:var(--ui)">{a}</td>
            </tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <h3>Labels that are not EPP statuses</h3>
    <p>
      Country-code registries publish their own vocabulary. You will meet these in a WHOIS
      answer, and a tool that treats them as standard statuses will misread them.
    </p>
    <div class="table-wrap" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="Registry-specific labels">
      <table class="listing-table" style="min-width:520px">
        <caption class="sr-only">Labels used by particular registries rather than standard EPP statuses</caption>
        <thead><tr><th>Label</th><th style="text-align:left">What it means</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          {ccLabels.map(([s, m]) => (
            <tr>
              <td class="dom">{s}</td>
              <td style="text-align:left; white-space:normal; font-family:var(--ui)">{m}</td>
            </tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p class="muted" style="margin-top:.8rem">
      A registry that publishes no status at all cannot be tracked this way, which is why
      coverage differs sharply between extensions.
    </p>

    <h2>How catching one works</h2>
    <p>
      You can try to register the name yourself at the moment of release, but on anything
      contested your odds are poor: the release is claimed in milliseconds by services that
      hold many simultaneous registry connections, often across several accredited registrars.
      What you do instead is place a <strong>backorder</strong>: you authorise a drop catcher to
      attempt the registration at release. Success is never guaranteed, and the pricing varies
      by provider: some charge only on success, others take a fee, a credit or a membership up
      front.
    </p>
    <p>
      If the service that wins has several customers wanting the same name, it commonly runs a
      private auction between them, so the final price is not simply the registration fee.
      Placing backorders with more than one service raises your odds and raises your cost.
      Budget for the catcher's fee and for that possible auction, not just for the registry
      price.
    </p>
    <p>
      Country-code extensions work differently from one registry to the next. Some run their
      own release process with a published queue, some hold a registry auction, and a few
      simply drop the name with no ceremony. A guide that tells you one universal rule for
      every extension is wrong.
    </p>

    <h2>Judging a name before you backorder it</h2>
    <p>
      The three routes to acquiring an expired name are compared in
      <a href="/guides/how-to-buy-expired-domains/">how to buy expired domains</a>. What is
      specific here is that you commit before you can inspect the name as a live registration,
      so the history is all you have.
      Check what the domain was with our <a href="/tools/whois-lookup/">WHOIS lookup</a> and
      <a href="/tools/domain-age-checker/"> domain age checker</a>, and read
      <a href="/guides/expired-domains-for-seo/">how to judge an expired domain</a> before you
      spend anything. A name that was a link farm is still a link farm after it drops.
    </p>
    <p>
      Our <a href="/">sample listings</a> span {tlds.length} extensions and show the metrics
      worth reading. They cover names at the auction and marketplace stage rather than the
      pending delete stage, which is tracked separately and on a weekly cycle.
    </p>
  </section>

  <CtaCatchDoms
    text="The tracked pending-delete inventory, by registry status and by days until the drop, lives on CatchDoms."
    label="Pending delete domains on CatchDoms"
    href="https://catchdoms.com/pending-delete-domains"
  />

  <section class="section faq">
    <h2>Questions</h2>
    {faqs.map((f) => (
      <details>
        <summary>{f.q}</summary>
        <p>{f.a}</p>
      </details>
    ))}
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
